+## 2. Drivers analysis
+
+### 2.1 Unreadable cohort breakdown (live measurement: 1,472 of 1,793 changes)
+
+| Cohort | Count | Share of unreadable | Driver class |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Legacy commits (pre-integrator, no integration record) | 1,161 | 78.9% | (a) writer gap |
+| Suite-status carrier commits (Act 21 records) | 307 | 20.9% | structural (records, not changes) |
+| Integrator landings missing git-anchored Act 26 record | 4 | 0.3% | (b)/(c) evidence policy / instrumentation |
+
+### 2.2 (a) Writer gaps — records reaching main without integration records
+
+Evidence: 1,161 of 1,472 unreadable commits are legacy commits (Oct–Nov 2026) that reached `main` with no preceding suite record, pre-push receipt, or Act 26 record. The checker's reason is uniformly "no preceding exact-commit/tree suite record". These commits predate the integrator (Act 31, ~Nov 18) and the Act 26 regime, so no preceding evidence was ever generated for them. Evidence strength: **measured** (checker output + git commit classification by message/date).
+
+Mechanism: `suite-status.sh` runs the suite and writes `records/suite-status.json`, but it is invoked by the integrator *during* landing (carrier commit *after* the candidate), so the record is post-hoc and never counts as "preceding". Preceding evidence can only come from (i) pre-push receipts (`reviewed-merge.sh` route, largely retired) or (ii) Act 26 pre-landing records. Legacy commits have neither.
+
+### 2.3 (b) Evidence policy gaps — records/registry fields missing
+
+Evidence: 4 integrator landings (`recovery-steward-assessment` ×2, `knowledge-statistics-preflight`, `certificate-frontier-results`) have Act 26 records in `state/delivery/` but are scored unreadable. `valid_act26_record` requires git-anchored copies on `main` (`main:records/investigations/<file>` + `.sha256`), which these landings lack (the integrator writes to `state/delivery/` only, never to `main`'s `records/investigations/`). Additionally, the known case "Act 26 record status field omitted" (`records/investigations/2026-11-08-act26-record-status-field-omitted.md`) shows required-field omissions occur. Of 309 `integration-*.json` in `state/delivery/`, only 127 are git-anchored on `main` (182 missing). Evidence strength: **measured** (file counts + checker behavior); the specific missing field per case is **claimed** (I did not inspect each record's fields).
+
+### 2.4 (c) Instrumentation gaps — branch diff, permission
+
+- The checker reads `state/delivery/*.json` (mode 0600, owner `lubko`) and git-anchored records; if the detached `.sha256` file or the `main:records/investigations/<file>` copy is absent, `valid_act26_record` returns `None` → unreadable.
+- 23 detached `.sha256` files exist in `delivery/` (vs 309 integration records) — a large instrumentation gap.
+- Seat worktrees (`viche-seats/branches/`, 230 branches) contain no suite-compliance instruments of their own; instrumentation is centralized in the town repo + `viche-state`.
+Evidence strength: **measured** (file counts).
+
+### 2.5 Strongest driver for the biggest unreadable cohort
+
+The biggest cohort (legacy, 1,161 of 1,472 = 78.9%) is driven by **writer gaps**: commits reaching `main` without any integration record, because they predate the evidence regime. Evidence strength: **measured**.
+
+Historical note: `records/investigations/2026-11-08-act26-landings-invisible-to-act21-checker.md` established that Act 26 landings were invisible to the Act 21 checker (the checker predated Act 26). The checker has since been extended to recognize Act 26 records (`valid_act26_record`), which is why 303 of 307 integrator landings are now compliant; the remaining 4 are the policy/instrumentation gap above.
+
+## 3. Cost-benefit ranking of candidate treatments
+
+Columns: problem % of signal lost | gap type | implementation cost | risk of false precision | reversibility. Ranked by marginal benefit/cost (assumptions in §3.1).
+
+| Rank | Treatment | Problem % of signal lost | Gap type | Impl. cost | Risk of false precision | Reversibility |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | **T2** Wire Act 26 records for every integration (integrator change): commit `records/investigations/<file>` + `.sha256` during landing | 182 of 309 Act 26 records (58.9%) not git-anchored; future landings at risk | evidence policy / instrumentation | medium | medium (byte-exact match required by checker) | high |
+| 2 | **T7** Exclude suite-status carrier commits from `per_change` | 307 of 1,472 unreadable rows (20.9%) are tautological (records, not changes) | measurement definition | low | medium (metric redefinition must be documented) | high |
+| 3 | **T1** Writer-side state-hash + `observed_at` → append-only transition manifest (writer change) | ~94% of snapshot bytes are duplicate content (529 distinct states of 8,708 files; ~146 MiB one-copy vs ~3.5 GiB) | storage / write-amp | medium | low (content-hash dedup is exact) | high |
+| 4 | **T3** Trim writer snapshots via Janitor quarantine (7-day grace) | stops growth of *stored* bytes; does **not** change coverage (coverage is computed at measurement time) | storage | low-medium | low | medium |
+| 5 | **T4** Increase checker detail on retries | red runs are rare (0 violations, 1 red_chronology_unresolved in current measurement) | instrumentation / diagnostic | low | low | high |
+| 6 | **T5** Add web push paging from missing detail files | missing detail files are not currently a problem (all 10,473 snapshots present) | reliability | medium-high | medium (could be noisy) | medium |
+| 7 | **T6** Backfill Act 26 records for the 1,161 legacy commits | 1,161 of 1,793 changes (64.7%) could become compliant | evidence policy | very high (manual review of 1,161 commits) | **high** (retroactive attestation undermines the evidence standard) | low |
+
+### 3.1 Assumptions for the ranking
+
+- (i) Benefit is measured against the current `main` snapshot (1,793 changes).
+- (ii) "Signal" = the compliance question (a preceding green suite record for an exact commit/tree).
+- (iii) Costs are implementation effort, not governance time.
+- (iv) Risk of false precision = chance the treatment makes the metric look better without real evidence improvement.
+- (v) T3 (trimming) does **not** make coverage go up or down: coverage is computed at measurement time from `main`'s commit history and the evidence files, not from the snapshot archive. Trimming removes per-file mtime evidence that 10,473 measurements ran, so auditability of "measurement ran" goes *down* even though the compliance question is unchanged.
+
+## 4. Honest limits, marked assumptions, predictions
+
+See §0.2 (assumptions A1–A6) and §0.3 (limits). Additional:
+
+- The "unreadable" label is a measurement artifact of the checker's evidence standard, **not** a compliance verdict on any officer or seat. I classified commits by message prefix and date; I did not grade individuals.
+- The aggregate snapshot-row rate (96.00% unreadable) is **not** a distinct-commit measure (A6); the distinct-commit rate on current `main` is 82.11%.
+- The predecessor report (Nov 18) recorded 98.71% unreadable across snapshots (7,102,820 / 7,195,706 rows). The measured rate now is 96.00% — the improvement reflects Act 26 records accumulating via integrator landings, not a change in the underlying standard. (Honest correction of a previously reported figure.)
+
+### Predictions (labeled)
+
+- **P1.** If T2 lands, the unreadable rate on *new* `main` commits → ~0 for integrator landings; the distinct-commit rate falls from 82.11% to ~65% (1,161/1,793) immediately; legacy commits remain unreadable.
+- **P2.** If T7 lands without a documented metric redefinition, reported unreadable drops to ~65% with **no** real evidence change — recommend against reporting it as progress.
+- **P3.** T3 (trimming) changes no unreadable row; it removes the per-file mtime evidence that 10,473 measurements ran.
+- **P4.** Absent T2, the unreadable rate falls ~1–2 percentage points per month at current landing rates as Act 26 records accumulate.
+- **P5.** Disk growth continues ~130–182 MiB/day until T1 (writer change) lands; T3 alone does not stop write volume, only stored volume.
